{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790342207,"nonce":"fz89/R1mbC2q5UFJjzMxPBi6ZcY=","title":"Freed compile transients stay resident: glibc keeps the peak, the Mac zone keeps fragments","message":"Compile scratch (lowering, SSA, Cranelift contexts, optimiser clones) shares the default malloc zone with long-lived HIR. On Linux nothing trims the heap after the SSA transient is freed; on the Mac the free space sits in partly used pages.\n\nEvidence. NUC, files with the capacity experiment: counted Rust heap 118 MB after lowering while VmRSS stays 742 MB. Mac print(1), vmmap: zylua 12.5 MB allocated with 4.5 MB fragmentation, zypy 4.47 with 5.4 (55 %); malloc_zone_pressure_relief at _exit released 0 bytes.\n\nSavings: Linux up to about 600 MB of RSS after a large compile (files); 3.5 to 5 MB of floor on the Mac.\n\nFix: malloc_trim(0) on Linux after the initial compile and after a large lowering; per-compile scratch in an arena released whole. Package MEM-release-transients.","files":null}]}